# A215 — the check pins one identity and starts an engine that uses another (2026-09-15T16:06:54Z)

## what the check does with the identity
$ grep -n "loadOrCreate\|argument" Sources/ChatBotsCore/TransportCheck.swift | head -8
  134:            identity = try CertificateStore.loadOrCreate(in: directory)
  135-        } catch {
  136-            report.recordFailure("certificate: \(error.localizedDescription)")
  137-            return report
  --
  169:        engineProcess.arguments = [
  170-            "--serve", "--transport", "webtransport",
  171-            "--transport-port", String(port),
  172-            // WebTransport only, so no HTTP listener is opened at all (A120). The unused port is
  (the identity comes from `directory`; the child is given ports and a transport, and nothing
   about certificates — so it loads or creates its own, usually .run/, and the pin cannot match)

## driving it from a test with a scratch identity
$ swift test --filter TransportCheckCoverageTests   # the passing path, before it was withdrawn
  fail: report.failures.isEmpty
  the report names what went wrong:
    certificate   15:B2:...:AE:91        <- generated, and pinned
    connected     NO
    round trips   0
    state         NOT RECEIVED
  failures: ["connect across processes: gave up after 30.0 seconds — Could not connect to the
             conversation engine: …"]

  The fingerprint line is the point: the check had an identity, the client pinned it, and the
  engine that came up did not have it. The check can only pass where `directory` happens to be
  the child's own default, which is the installer's case and not a property of the check.

  A second half of this was fixed while investigating (see A170): the executable defaulted to
  `ProcessInfo.arguments.first`, which under `swift test` is the SwiftPM testing helper rather
  than an engine at all. It is an injectable parameter now, and the failure above is what remains
  once the engine is named correctly.
